Bodies have a way of keeping score even when we might not even care about the score. Friday, November 13th is my last day as a Product Manager at RedZone. I started in February.  

In the short amount of time (8 months) that I've been here, I've done some work I'm really proud of. I stood up an ERP (enterprise resource planning/platform) for the service desk in my first couple of months with a tool I was not familiar with. There have been other wins too, but that by far the work I'm most proud of. 

But something weird started to happen in May. It's like my brain was glitchy. 

I knew the last few years have been stressful and I've tried to adapt my self-care to it.  What I didn't realize is that there were some lingering effects. So as my work stress increased in an environment that isn't a great match for my style, my body started to get my attention. I just thought, "I just need to get past X, then everything will get better." But it didn't. I couldn't remember things that I knew. I would freeze. I couldn't apply my normal way of working to the situation. I couldn't handle the stress.  And that's frustrating. Because I've been yelled at in meetings with Boards of Directors. I juggled the stress of raising kids, growing my career, doing fitness competitions, long distance running, and founding & organizing a global virtual conference, and that was pretty much fine.  

But now? My brain's like: let's be like AI. Sometimes good, sometimes slop. I also noticed similarities in how I responded to other stressful events, which was very fascinating. 

My brain started to catch up with my body in late August when the company's values changed. I thought to myself: this feels different and not in a great way, but I put it out of my mind and got back to work.  Then over a weekend in October, I was talking to a Product Management Career Coach, Sam Greenwood. During the course of our conversation,I shared the idea I had before I started at RedZone; data driven patient advocacy.  I lit up when I was talking about it.  I reflected on that. Listened to my body as I said various statements out loud. And that was my deciding point. 

After that weekend, I came back to the office and shared with my boss that I think it's best for everyone if I move on. I have never left a job so soon.  It's sad and exciting at the same time. I enjoy working with the people I work with. I enjoy my work, for the most part. 

But here's what I know:
⓵ I can't work in a place where my body is keeping score so meticulously.
⓶ It's okay if it's not a good fit.  I felt momentary shame. That "you can't cut it" shame that is so prevalent in tech. Then I got over it because who TF cares?
⓷ Great leaders are those that take care of their people and are productive.

